The code statements that lead to the bug are:
cur_len = data[i];
if (data[i + 1] == EIR_FLAGS && ...)
if (i + cur_len >= len) return false; // placed after type checks

Reason:
The function checks the type byte (data[i + 1]) before verifying that the current field actually fits within the buffer. A malformed advertising TLV where the length byte is the last byte of the buffer causes `cur_len = data[i]` to be set to some value, but `data[i + 1]` reads one byte past the end because `i + 1` equals the buffer length. The length check `if (i + cur_len >= len)` is only performed after the type access, so it cannot prevent the out-of-bounds read.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (i + cur_len >= len) return false;
// moved before type checks

Reason:
By placing the length validation immediately after reading `cur_len` (and after the `if (!cur_len) continue;` skip), the fix ensures that any non‑empty field is proven to contain its type byte before the parser accesses `data[i + 1]`. If `i + cur_len >= len`, the field exceeds the buffer and the function returns false without reading out of bounds. This prevents the KASAN‑reported vulnerability.

